VERDICT AT THE SHAPE THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS
===========================================
At 56x56 (ResNet50's real conv2_x, not a compile-constrained stand-in): NPU hw
4.2435 ms vs CPU 0.3327 ms -- CPU wins 12.75x, worse than the 5.7-11.4x range found
across the compile-limited 32-wide shapes in bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log.
Marginal (steady-state, fixed-cost-removed) rate: NPU 111.1 GOPS vs CPU 1678.8 GOPS
-- 15.1x. This closes the item bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log left explicitly open:
"w > 32 for this op. Untested, because untestable without rewriting the design's
buffering. 56x56 -- the shape that actually matters for ResNet50 -- is on the far
side of that rewrite." It is no longer untested: reaching the real shape did not
narrow the gap, it widened it. The CLOSED verdict (int8 chained ResNet-bottleneck
convolution on XDNA1 via this mlir-aie design loses to Zen4 CPU at every shape) now
includes the shape that mattered most, and loses by more there than anywhere else
measured.

CAVEAT
======
This NPU fit is 2 points on the w-axis (32x56, 56x56), not the 5-point h-axis fit in
bottleneck_spatial_sweep_npu.log (146.1 GOPS marginal) -- different axis, not
directly the same quantity, though both read as "far below the CPU." Single-
buffering the output FIFO trades throughput for fit (compute stalls until the
previous row's DMA-out drains) -- part of why marginal GOPS here (111.1) reads
lower than the h-axis sweep's 146.1, not evidence of a regression in the width fix
itself. A future column-scaled or double-buffered-output design could plausibly
close some of the gap on GOPS alone; it would need to close a lot more of it than
1.7x (the widest previously-open estimate) to change the verdict, and column count
was already the more promising untested lever going in.

Local mlir-aie checkout only (bottleneck.py's depth=1 change, plus the width fixes
it depends on); never upstreamed, per project convention.
